y in this  work but in many other places as well  just think about the pleasure of taste  he contrasts human beings to other  animals other animals like to eat this  or they like to eat that they enjoy this  thing or that thing we already actually  omnivores you know we're we're not only  sort of a universal kinda animal with  respect to building you know technology  and tools and use of the mind and reason  and all that sort of stuff we're also  universal the fact that will eat just  about anything in front of mate or  another and generally find some way to  make it tasty as a matter of fact in  this very section Aristotle mentions the  art of cookery as one that we could say  is an art designed to give pleasure  right but then let's sort of a scientist  you now some pleasures Aristotle thinks  are are better than other pleasures and  he talks about this in terms of them  being more noble this word noble that  I've got your cologne you know being  fine also it's important to point out  that in other places he'll talk about  the noble or the fine person as being  the genuine criterion for what is what  is genuinely pleasant hill talking about  some pleasures being naturally pleasant  but not being naturally Pleasant in that  same way necessarily to everybody  because they haven't you know  sufficiently appreciated those pleasures  those are pleasures that don't involve  pain in some way and to understand what  he means by that let's think about what  he calls restorative pleasures quite a  few of the pleasures that we experience  involve a kind of setting things back  into equilibrium setting things back  into balance consumption of food  consumption of liquid when we're thirsty  great examples of this sleeping in on a  on a Saturday morning perhaps even into  the afternoon because we've got a long  week of work  and not getting enough sleep that could  be another example of restorative  pleasure Aristotle tends to think about  sexual activity and desire pleasure  probably orgasm things like that along  those same lines as well and we might  come up with many other examples  Aristotle also thinks that it's possible  for a person to need in the sense of  that restorative pleasure things that  are naturally that way so you know when  we talk about somebody experiencing  addiction that could be alarmed along  those sorts of lines you know think  about you know smoking tobacco right  when you first do it you get this great  rush and feels like your lungs are you  know scrunching in on themselves and all  sorts of cool things happen your body  after a while that first cigarette in  the day may still be very pleasant taste  good but you really want it because it  you need it you needed to restore or  something that's not there coffee  caffeine addiction is like that as well  I can quite attest to that myself having  tried to wean myself off before and not  having been particularly successful so  Aristotle says that the pleasures of the  body is a little bit earlier on in the  discussion are the ones that were most  often familiar with and so he talks  about them you surfing the name of  pleasure that what he means by that is  they become for many people the paradigm  for how we understand pleasure we think  of pleasures primarily in terms of  bodily pleasures well you know what we  put in our mouths right what we do with  our mouths you know what we do with our  body or sense of touch we can think of  other pleasures as well along these  lines but bodily pleasures are the ones  Aristotle thinks that were most familiar  with so Aristotle suggests that  necessary pleasures and bodily pleasures  are examples of necessary pleasures per  tick  early baccano restorative pleasures they  are good in the sense of being knock  that right the opposite of them the pain  is actually bad maybe we want to say  well they're not great they're not  particularly good these pleasures but  insofar as they're relieving pain  they're they're good right word perhaps  they're good up to a certain point  perhaps having a glass of wine with  dinner and appreciating that glass of  wine is a good pleasure to engage it  having you know one or two drinks you  know at the party as opposed to drinking  you know 12 drinks and then you know  hectoring the bartenders until they they  cut you off difference there right you  know this is a typically Greek thing  everything in moderation Aristotle is  all about that so perhaps bodily  pleasures are in fact good for us  insofar as we enjoy them up to a certain  point now he says that's probably the  right way to look at things but we also  want to understand why people go wrong  with this why do people pursue bodily  pleasures so exclusively or so  single-mindedly to the detriment of the  other pleasures that they could be  enjoying which would in fact be better  for them they wouldn't entail pain of  some sort they having nobler they would  elevate them perhaps you know why do  people think that they should have the  lower pleasures and deprive themselves  of the higher ones so Aristotle says  here's two possible reasons for that one  is that pleasure drives out pain so  sometimes we want pleasure not for its  own sake but because pleasure will help  us do not feel a kind of pain anymore  and here he's talking about restorative  pleasures right so again think about it  when you're thirsty you really want to  drink something you probably don't even  you literally care what it is you'll  you'll drink an iced tea that you  ordinarily would never purchase because  somebody hands it to your like drink  something right and then afterwards  you're like can't believe I drink that  that garbage right so we seek bodily  pleasures as restorative I think you  could also make a case that we seek them  as what we might call compensatory so  when we're feeling pain in one respect  doesn't even necessarily have to be a  physical pain it could be psychological  pain then we often seek out bodily  p